sion, as insane as that might sound as for the True Fae.<br /> <br /> See, the True Fae, for all their power and knowledge, are effectively birds in their eggs. Changeling-born Strangers are actively the result of a character who just couldn't handle reality anymore, who decided to run away from people and pain and life, and retreat into the safety and security of fantasy.* In their Arcadian coffins, they are all powerful, they have friends who will never betray them, they have vistas that appeal to them, they have a perfect life. They cut off their wings and curl back up in the egg they found warmest. Arcadian-born Strangers skip some of the shit talking I'm giving here, but have a fundamental disconnect in that the Others have no real sense of the Other(heh) to begin with. For both, that's fine to begin with, at least from an in-house perspective, but anyone who has dreamt up characters and chronicles for RPG's without a group to play them with will know that that comes to be worthless after a while, and this carefully constructed masturbation of a world loses its savor until even that fades, and with how much is invested in them, the True Fae are likely to go with it...if it weren't for the fact that most of them realize that there's something beyond their borders.<br /> <br /> This concept of life beyond themselves is alien and troubling, in some ways, and it results in the Legends that True Fae engage, at least in low numbers of Titles. It's conflict, it's the clashing of ideals and fantasies until one falls into submission to the other, and this sense of a world outside of yourself is brought inside. You make the Other the Self. But in doing so, a True Fae is going to find a new addiction. See, there is now a new fantasy with interesting parameters and imaginary friends. There's a <i>life</i> beyond their initial fantasy. Sure, they still don't want to go beyond their egg, but they can bring things in. So they get in fights with people with conflicting viewpoints, and in doing so expand their worlds and their modes of fun and pleasure. In short, increasing in Titles is an increase in awareness of a life they can live beyond themselves.<br /> <br /> So, what do <i>I </i>think happens to a True Fae who Transcends? Easy. I think they reincarnate into human souls, to break out of the egg once again and stretch their wings, trying the storms and forests and people of the real world once again.<br /> <br /> Now, none of this is canon, mind you. The question of what happens to a True Fae who Transcends is honestly too damn large for any one person to answer. It's something play groups should define for themselves-I, at least, would never really want to write a section suggesting a canon answer. But for me, this makes logical sense. On top of that, it makes <i>thematic</i> sense. Changeling is a game about recovery, and at it's core is the ideal of hope. I like the idea that, no matter how far you fall, you always have the chance to become really, truly, okay. <br /> <br /> *Actually, I want to clarify since this has real world mirrors, I don't actually object to this so long as the person is making a conscious choice to surrender to their world in full knowledge and awareness of the consequences. A person has the right to choose the world they live in, and the things they sacrifice for it-as an example, if someone wants to just live their life glued to the internet, they can, so long as they accept that such a choice means abandoning, say, a spouse and child in the real world. That might be a little sad, but I do firmly believe that people have a right to their choice of world as much as anything else. It can't just be addiction or trouble, it has to be choice though.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a really cool way to interpret the transcendence,I always like you changelings essays. Oh but since you mentioned the becoming nightmare process,do you think a changeling becoming far would retain some of his personality and attachments?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-13T21:39:52-08:00","id":829573,"title":"","content":"Becoming Nightmare is...regressive, to say the least. I think it more likely that personality and attachments become set pieces and bits of attire. Like, a changeling-born True Fae won't still be in love with her wife from before*, but don't be too surprised when you go the realm and the dread consort of the Stranger reminds you of her.<br /> <br /> *Unless, of course she does, but remember that True Fae find reality to be too much to handle. Real people with real emotions and real problems suffocate them. A True Fae might love a person they once knew in the same way a kid loves the weekly toy they get. It might be super intense for a bit, but it has no real staying power.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-13T21:43:16-08:00","id":829575,"title":"","content":"Sweet those guys are such cool villains","signature":"","edi